course: dictionary
lesson: 11761
title: Words with a history
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word barbarian has more than one meaning, and the sentence around it tells you which one is in use. In a history book, «The barbarians attacked the Roman Empire» means outside groups, such as the Goths, Vandals and Huns, whom the Romans saw as wild.
explain: In everyday talk, «He pushed to the front and ate all the cake. What a barbarian!» is a joke about bad manners. Ask yourself: is this about history, cruelty, or manners?
explain: Some words carry an opinion. The Romans used barbarian for people from other cultures, so the word can be unfair. When you read it, ask who is using the word and what they think of the people they are describing.
fact: Barbarian can be a noun (a barbarian, barbarians) or an adjective, as in «barbarian tribes».
fact: «Barbarian» means a person who behaves in a very cruel, violent or rough way.
fact: The ancient Greeks and Romans used barbarian for people from outside their world, whom they saw as wild and uncivilised.
fact: In history, the Goths, Vandals and Huns were called barbarians, and they attacked the Roman Empire.
fact: In a joke, «You put ketchup on that? You barbarian!» means you have bad manners or no taste.
exercise: «The barbarians attacked the Roman Empire.» What does barbarians mean here? | Students learning Latin at school | *Outside groups the Romans saw as wild and uncivilised | Players in a football team | Peaceful traders who sold spices
exercise: Ella says, «You put ketchup on your pancakes? You barbarian!» What does she mean? | *You have bad manners or no taste | You are a fierce warrior | You are a history teacher | You are very old and wise
exercise: «The soldiers were barbarian in their cruelty to the village.» What does barbarian suggest? | Clever with numbers | Kind and gentle | *Very cruel, violent or rough | Very quiet and shy
test: «The city was in danger, with barbarians at the gates.» What does at the gates mean? | *Enemies close by | Friendly guests waiting to come in | Children playing outside the school | Farmers selling vegetables
test: «The Romans called the Goths barbarians, but the Goths had their own rich culture.» Why might this word be unfair? | The Romans liked the Goths very much | *The Romans judged people from other cultures as wild, when they were not | The Goths had no culture at all | The Romans and Goths were the same people
exam: Tom reads, «The barbarians rode into the village, burning the houses.» Which meaning fits? | *A cruel, violent person | A kind teacher | A person who is always late | Someone who is very polite
